// One number, and the only one that works.
//
// A growing tip puts out leaves one at a time, turning a little between each.  How far it turns
// decides everything about the plant: whether the leaves end up in tidy rows with the upper ones
// shading the lower, or spread so that every one of them gets light.  Real plants -- sunflowers,
// pine cones, pineapples, the scales on a fir -- turn by very nearly the same angle, and it is not a
// round number.
//
// **It is 360 degrees times tau to the minus two, about 137.5**, where tau is the golden mean.  The
// three heads here are the same construction with only that angle changed.
//
// **Left, 90 degrees.**  A quarter turn, so every fourth floret lands in the same direction and the
// head is four spokes with nothing between them.  Any angle that is a simple fraction of a turn does
// this: the pattern closes on itself and the gaps never fill.
//
// **Middle, 137.0 degrees.**  Half a degree short of the golden angle, which sounds close enough.
// It is not: the arms curve away into visible spirals with lanes of bare ground between them.
//
// **Right, 137.507... degrees.**  No two florets ever line up, because the golden mean is the number
// hardest to approximate by a fraction -- that is what being "most irrational" means, and it is
// exactly the property wanted here.  The head packs evenly all the way out, and the spirals your eye
// picks out run in both directions at once, in counts that are consecutive Fibonacci numbers.
//
// Each floret sits at a distance proportional to the square root of its number, which is what keeps
// the area they share constant: the head grows outward at the rate its middle produces them.
//
// The construction is nothing but a loop, a square root, and a turn.  There is no engine support for
// any of this and none is needed -- which is worth saying, because it looks like it ought to need
// some.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    info {
        title 'The Angle a Plant Uses'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'One head built three times, turning by 90, 137 and 137.5 degrees between florets.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

// Looking down on the heads, but not straight down: a camera aimed along the world's own up axis has
// no way left to decide which way is up in the picture, and renders very little.
camera {
    location [0, 11.5, -9.8]
    look at [0, -0.3, 0.2]
    field of view 56
}

point light { location [6, 12, -9] }

point light {
    location [0, 11.5, -9.8]
    color [0.26, 0.28, 0.34]
}

background [0.09, 0.10, 0.13]

Floret = material {
    pigment [0.46, 0.34, 0.17]
    specular 0.45
    shininess 80
    ambient 0.14
}

Rim = material {
    pigment [0.62, 0.50, 0.20]
    specular 0.30
    shininess 50
    ambient 0.12
}

// One head: florets numbered outward, each turned by the given angle from the one before it and set
// at the square root of its number.
primitive Head(angle, count, spacing) -> group {
    return group {
        for i in [1, count] {
            sphere {
                material Floret
                scale spacing * 0.46
                // A floret is flattened, because a seed lying in a head is not a ball.
                scale [1, 0.55, 1]
                translate X (spacing * sqrt(i))
                rotate Y (i * angle)
            }
        }

        // A rim, so each head reads as a head rather than as a scatter of dots.
        for edge in [0, 71] {
            sphere {
                material Rim
                scale spacing * 0.40
                scale [1, 0.5, 1]
                translate X (spacing * sqrt(count) + spacing * 0.75)
                rotate Y (edge * 5)
            }
        }
    }
}

object Head(90, 300, 0.145)         { translate [-5.8, 0, -3.4] }
object Head(137.0, 300, 0.145)      { }
object Head(137.507764, 300, 0.145) { translate [5.8, 0, 3.4] }
